-- Where an `@nlg` herald may sit relative to a rule's head, and which consumer
-- reads what. smucclaw/l4-ide#972.
--
-- The rule PR #433 landed is that an annotation attaches to what it sits with:
-- trailing a line it describes what is on that line, starting a line of its
-- own it describes what follows. What that left unsaid is what happens when
-- the head carries an INPUT, because then the last thing the herald sits with
-- may be the input rather than the rule. The appform argument is that input's
-- BINDING occurrence (L4.TypeCheck.ensureNameConsistency does `def` on it, not
-- `mkref`), so the herald lands on the input.
--
-- WHAT THE TWO PROJECTIONS USED TO DO ABOUT THAT, and why it was a defect
-- rather than a placement rule. `l4 render` reads such a herald as the RULE's
-- sentence, on purpose -- L4.Export.Document.decideNlg searches the appform's
-- arguments and its comment says so. `l4 nlg` did not, because its Linearize
-- instance for Resolved sees only the Name a call site resolves to, with no way
-- back up to the enclosing DECIDE. Same bytes, two answers: measured over the
-- nine modules of canon's il/ofek-hadash-2008/encodings/legalese, 65 heralds
-- written, 65 read by `l4 render`, 0 by `l4 nlg`.
--
-- L4.Nlg.promoteHeadInputNlg now MOVES such a herald onto the rule before
-- linearizing, using the same L4.Nlg.decideNlg lookup `l4 render` uses, so
-- every row below reads the same in both projections. It moves rather than
-- copies: left on the input as well, the sentence printed twice in one line at
-- a named-argument call site, once as the heading and again as the input's
-- gloss.
--
-- Measured 2026-09-21, one rule per row. Every row carries BOTH a positional
-- and a `WITH` directive, because the loss used to be per CALL SITE -- a
-- positional call printed the bare name while a `WITH` call printed the
-- sentence as the input's gloss -- and the `.nlg.golden` beside this file is
-- what pins that the two columns now agree.
--
--   #  head shape                      herald placement        nlg positional  nlg WITH   render
--   1  DECIDE `r` IF ...               trailing the head       herald          herald     herald
--   2  DECIDE `r` IF ...               own line above          herald          herald     herald
--   3  `r` MEANS ...                   trailing the head       herald          herald     herald
--   4  `r` MEANS ...                   own line above          herald          herald     herald
--   5  `r` p MEANS ...                 trailing the head       herald          herald     herald
--   6  `r` p MEANS ...                 own line above          herald          herald     herald
--   7  `r` p / herald / MEANS ...      own line BELOW the p    herald          herald     herald
--   8  DECIDE `r` p IS ...             trailing the head       herald          herald     herald
--   9  `r` / herald / p MEANS ...      own line ABOVE the p    herald          herald     herald
--  10  `r` p AKA `a` / herald / MEANS  own line below the AKA  herald          herald     BARE
--  11  herald / `r` p AKA `a` MEANS    own line above          BARE NAME       BARE NAME  herald
--  12  `r` p q / herald / MEANS ...    own line below the q    herald          herald     herald
--  13  herald / `r` p / herald / MEANS both                    OUTER           OUTER      OUTER
--
-- Rows 5, 7, 8 and 12 are the repair. Before it they printed the bare rule name
-- positionally; rows 1-4, 6 and 9 already read the herald and are unchanged,
-- which is the point of keeping them here -- the fix must not disturb the
-- placements that already worked.
--
-- Three things the table settles, and none is what you would guess.
--
-- (a) The variable was never `DECIDE` versus `MEANS`, and it was never trailing
-- versus own line. Rows 9 and 6 both put the herald on a line of its own and
-- both always reached the rule; row 9 does it from INSIDE the head, above the
-- input. The condition was which name the herald was written after, and the
-- repair is that it no longer matters.
--
-- (b) Row 13 is why the repair cannot be "always promote". A rule that heralds
-- ITSELF above its head keeps that sentence: L4.Nlg.decideNlg finds the rule's
-- own annotation before it reaches the appform arguments, so nothing is moved
-- and the inner herald stays the input's gloss. An author who writes both meant
-- two different things.
--
-- (c) An `AKA` on the head is a SEPARATE axis and this change does not touch
-- it. Row 10 reaches `l4 nlg` (the AKA's name claims the herald) and not
-- `l4 render`; row 11 reaches `l4 render` and not `l4 nlg`. So on an `AKA` head
-- there is still no placement that satisfies both. That is not #972 -- the
-- herald there is not on an appform argument -- and it needs its own issue.
--
-- The prelude writes row 5's shape 67 times (`null list @nlg %list% is empty`),
-- so those 67 sentences reach a positional `l4 nlg` call for the first time
-- under this change. That is the intended effect, not a side effect.

-- Row 1: DECIDE, trailing the head.
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `row one` @nlg row one saw %amount%
  IF amount GREATER THAN 100

#EVAL `row one` 200
#EVAL `row one` WITH amount IS 200

-- Row 2: DECIDE, own line above the head.
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
@nlg row two saw %amount%
DECIDE `row two` IF amount GREATER THAN 100

#EVAL `row two` 200
#EVAL `row two` WITH amount IS 200

-- Row 3: MEANS, trailing the head.
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`row three` @nlg row three saw %amount%
  MEANS amount GREATER THAN 100

#EVAL `row three` 200
#EVAL `row three` WITH amount IS 200

-- Row 4: MEANS, own line above the head.
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
@nlg row four saw %amount%
`row four` MEANS amount GREATER THAN 100

#EVAL `row four` 200
#EVAL `row four` WITH amount IS 200

-- Row 5: MEANS with the input in the head, herald trailing. The prelude's own
-- shape, 67 times over. The herald lands on `amount` and is moved to the rule.
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`row five` amount @nlg row five saw %amount%
  MEANS amount GREATER THAN 100

#EVAL `row five` 200
#EVAL `row five` WITH amount IS 200

-- Row 6: input in the head, herald on its own line ABOVE the head. This is the
-- placement the reference page recommends, and rows 5, 7, 8 and 12 now render
-- identically to it.
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
@nlg row six saw %amount%
`row six` amount MEANS amount GREATER THAN 100

#EVAL `row six` 200
#EVAL `row six` WITH amount IS 200

-- Row 7: the input in the head, herald on a line of its own BELOW it. The shape
-- every one of the 65 heralds in canon's Ofek Hadash encoding uses, and the
-- shape the issue was filed about.
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`row seven` amount
    @nlg row seven saw %amount%
    MEANS amount GREATER THAN 100

#EVAL `row seven` 200
#EVAL `row seven` WITH amount IS 200

-- Row 8: the same shape as row 5 under the `DECIDE ... IS` spelling, which is
-- why neither the repair nor this table keys on the keyword.
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `row eight` amount @nlg row eight saw %amount%
  IS amount TIMES 0.1

#EVAL `row eight` 200
#EVAL `row eight` WITH amount IS 200

-- Row 9: INSIDE the head but ABOVE the input. This is the counterexample to
-- "an annotation inside a head describes the input": the herald is written
-- after the rule's name and before the input, so the rule claimed it all along
-- and nothing is moved here.
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`row nine`
    @nlg row nine saw %amount%
    amount
    MEANS amount GREATER THAN 100

#EVAL `row nine` 200
#EVAL `row nine` WITH amount IS 200

-- Row 10: an AKA on the head, herald on a line of its own below it. The AKA's
-- name claims the herald, so it is not on an appform argument, nothing is
-- moved, and the pre-existing asymmetry stands: `l4 nlg` prints it and
-- `l4 render` prints its own paraphrase. Pinned so that a later change to the
-- AKA case has to notice this file.
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`row ten` amount AKA `row ten alias`
    @nlg row ten saw %amount%
    MEANS amount GREATER THAN 100

#EVAL `row ten` 200
#EVAL `row ten` WITH amount IS 200

-- Row 11: the same AKA head with the herald ABOVE it. `l4 render` finds it, and
-- so does `l4 nlg` -- not through the head-input repair (the herald is on no
-- appform argument) but through the directive splice, which reads a herald
-- wherever `decideNlg` finds one and fills its slots from the call. Row 10 is
-- now the one row in this table where the two projections still disagree.
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
@nlg row eleven saw %amount%
`row eleven` amount AKA `row eleven alias` MEANS amount GREATER THAN 100

#EVAL `row eleven` 200
#EVAL `row eleven` WITH amount IS 200

-- Row 12: TWO inputs. The herald attaches to the LAST one, and that is the one
-- moved. Worth pinning, because "the input" is ambiguous in a head that carries
-- several, and because the `where` clause below shows both inputs recovering
-- their own names once the sentence has moved off the second one.
GIVEN floor IS A NUMBER
      amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`row twelve` floor amount
    @nlg row twelve saw %amount% over %floor%
    MEANS amount GREATER THAN floor

#EVAL `row twelve` 100 200
#EVAL `row twelve` WITH floor IS 100, amount IS 200

-- Row 13: the rule ALSO heralds itself above its head. The rule's own sentence
-- wins in both projections and the inner herald stays the input's gloss, so an
-- author who writes both still gets both. This is the case that makes the
-- repair conditional rather than unconditional.
GIVEN amount IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
@nlg row thirteen the rule saw %amount%
`row thirteen` amount
    @nlg row thirteen the input
    MEANS amount GREATER THAN 100

#EVAL `row thirteen` 200
#EVAL `row thirteen` WITH amount IS 200
